---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Large-Scale Structure"
  description="Studies of the cosmic web — clusters, filaments, voids, and protoclusters — at the UCR Extragalactic Astrophysics Group."
  path="/pages/science/lss.html"
>
<div class="container">
<div id="lss" class="page active">
            <a href="../science.html" class="back-btn">← Back to Science</a>

            <div class="content-section">
                <h2>Large Scale Structures</h2>

                <p class="intro-text">
                    Using galaxy surveys, we have developed techniques to identify large scale
                    structures in the Universe. This includes clusters, filaments, voids, outskirts of
                    clusters, and nodes (the intersection between two filaments). We use photometric
                    redshifts to build the Large Scale Structures as a function of redshift in the
                    range 0.5 &lt; z &lt; 6. This allows a study of environmental dependence of
                    properties of galaxies, color and morphological properties of galaxies in
                    filaments and clusters, identification and study of proto-clusters, environmental
                    quenching, and many other studies.
                </p>

                <!-- Identification of the Large Scale Structure -->
                <div class="research-detail-card">
                    <h3><span class="icon"></span> Identification of the Large Scale Structure</h3>

                    <div class="figure-container">
                        <img src="../../assets/images/lss/overdensity-maps.png"
                             alt="Evolution of overdensity maps in the COSMOS-Web field">
                        <p class="figure-caption">
                            Evolution with cosmic time of overdensity maps in the COSMOS-Web field.
                            Each panel shows the overdensity for a fixed comoving slice. Color bars
                            are scaled independently in each redshift bin. The maps show changing
                            topology and distribution of large scale structure with redshift. Circles
                            show the star-masked regions
                            (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10727"
                                target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hatamnia et al. 2026</a>).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Mass-Metallicity Relation in different environments -->
                <div class="research-detail-card">
                    <h3><span class="icon"></span> Mass–Metallicity Relation in different environments</h3>

                    <div class="figure-container">
                        <img src="../../assets/images/lss/mzr-environments.png"
                             alt="Mass-metallicity relation in three environments at z~1.5 and z~2.3">
                        <p class="figure-caption">
                            MZR relation at z~1.5 (left) and z~2.3 (right) for galaxies residing in
                            three different environments: overdensity (red), intermediate density
                            (green), under-dense (blue). For [N II]6584 non-detections, metallicities
                            are shown by inverted triangles. The fitted average MZR with its 1σ error
                            is shown by the shaded area. Metallicity at a given stellar mass is
                            higher in denser environments at z~1.5 while the trend reverses at z~2.3,
                            so that galaxies in denser environments have lower metallicities. The
                            SDSS local MZR is shown by the solid grey line and the average MZR for
                            all the MOSDEF galaxies by the dashed grey line
                            (<a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2021ApJ...908..120C/abstract"
                                target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chartab et al. 2022</a>).
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Selected publications -->
                <div class="research-detail-card">
                    <h3><span class="icon"></span> Selected Publications</h3>
                    <ul class="lss-pub-list">
                        <li>
                            <a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016ApJ...825..113D/abstract"
                               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                Darvish, B., Mobasher, B. et al. 2016
                            </a>
                            — “The Effects of the Local Environment and Stellar Mass on Galaxy
                            Quenching to z ∼ 3,” ApJ 825, 113.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2015ApJ...805..121D/abstract"
                               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                Darvish, B., Mobasher, B. et al. 2015
                            </a>
                            — “A Comprehensive Study of Density Field Estimation: A New Insight in
                            the Evolution of Galaxies with Environment in COSMOS to z ∼ 3,” ApJ 805,
                            121.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2021ApJ...908..120C/abstract"
                               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                Chartab, N., Mobasher, B. et al. 2021
                            </a>
                            — “MOSDEF Survey: Environmental Dependence of the Gas Phase Metallicity
                            of Galaxies at 1.4 &lt; z &lt; 2.6,” ApJ 908, 120.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2024ApJ...966...18T/abstract"
                               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                Taamoli, S., Mobasher, B. et al. 2024
                            </a>
                            — “Large-Scale Structures in COSMOS2020: Evolution of Star Formation
                            Activity in Different Environments at 0.4 &lt; z &lt; 4,” ApJ 966, 18.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2024ApJ...977..263T/abstract"
                               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                Taamoli, S., Mobasher, B. et al. 2024
                            </a>
                            — “COSMOS2020: Disentangling the Role of Mass and Environment in Star
                            Formation Activity of Galaxies at 0.4 &lt; z &lt; 4,” ApJ 977, 263.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.10727"
                               target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                                Hatamnia, H., Mobasher, B. et al. 2026
                            </a>
                            — “Large-Scale Structure in COSMOS-Web: Tracing Galaxy Evolution in the
                            Cosmic Web up to z ∼ 7 with the Largest JWST Survey,” arXiv:2511.10727.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

            </div>
        </div>
</div>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
    .intro-text {
        color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
        font-size: 1.05rem;
        line-height: 1.8;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    .research-detail-card {
        background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(20, 30, 60, 0.9), rgba(10, 20, 50, 0.95));
        border: 1px solid rgba(100, 150, 255, 0.25);
        border-radius: 16px;
        padding: 2.5rem;
        margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
        position: relative;
        overflow: hidden;
    }
    .research-detail-card::before {
        content: '';
        position: absolute;
        top: 0; left: 0; right: 0;
        height: 4px;
        background: linear-gradient(90deg, #4a90d9, #8b5cf6, #06b6d4);
        opacity: 0.8;
    }
    .research-detail-card h3 {
        color: #7dd3fc;
        font-size: 1.6rem;
        margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.75rem;
    }

    .figure-container {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
    }
    .figure-container img {
        width: 100%;
        max-width: 1000px;
        display: block;
        margin: 0 auto;
        border-radius: 8px;
        background: #fff;
    }
    .figure-caption {
        color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.75);
        font-size: 0.95rem;
        line-height: 1.55;
        margin-top: 0.75rem;
        text-align: justify;
    }
    .figure-caption a {
        color: #7dd3fc;
        text-decoration: none;
    }
    .figure-caption a:hover,
    .figure-caption a:focus-visible {
        color: #0ff;
        text-decoration: underline;
        outline: none;
    }

    .lss-pub-list {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0;
        margin: 0;
        color: #cbd5e1;
    }
    .lss-pub-list li {
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
        line-height: 1.55;
    }
    .lss-pub-list a {
        color: #7dd3fc;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: 500;
    }
    .lss-pub-list a:hover,
    .lss-pub-list a:focus-visible {
        color: #0ff;
        text-decoration: underline;
        outline: none;
    }
</style>
